Title: Migrate memory bodies to the common format (OKF-compatible frontmatter)
Created: 2026-09-10
Author: claude
Blocked-by: 0917

--- log ---
2026-09-10T15:50Z claude created

--- body ---

## Context

Ranking reads frontmatter, and the frontmatter is neither uniform nor rich
enough to rank on. Across roughly 954 bodies: 47 carry no type at all, 208 use
a top-level `type:`, 746 use `metadata.type`, and none carries a title, a
timestamp or tags. The index holds the human-readable title, which is backwards
— the index is a generated view, so everything it displays must come from the
bodies.

The target format is settled: **Markdown with YAML frontmatter on the OKF v0.1
field names** — `type`, `title`, `description`, `tags`, `timestamp` — which
costs nothing, since those are the fields ranking needs anyway.

**The canonical form is the portable one.** Runtime-specific spellings are
generated aliases, never the source. So `name:` is dropped (the filename is the
slug, and the runtime derives one from the path when the field is absent), and
`type` sits flat rather than nested under `metadata:`. `description` stays
because the convention and the runtime agree on it — where they agree there is
no idiosyncrasy to remove.

This ticket is **only** the format and the migration. The grammar, the
resolver, `supersedes` and the scorer are `0914`; the quality dimensions are
`0919`; the dates that fill `timestamp` come from `0917`, which must merge
first.

## The index filename, decided

**Two indexes, different content.** `index.md` is the **complete** catalogue —
one line per live body, no cut. `MEMORY.md` is the **budgeted** view — the top
N, whose overflow line points at `index.md`. The runtime loads the second by
that name and `tests/test_resident_census.py` budgets it under that name, so
dropping it would remove the resident index from the system prompt in silence.
The first is what the store publishes and what an adapter on another runtime
reads.

**Open, and this ticket's to settle:** whether a folder index in the target
convention is expected to be complete. If it is, the split above is already
right. If a partial index is admissible, the split is still right, because the
two views have different jobs — but say which reading the schema follows rather
than leaving a reader to infer it.

## Relevant files

- `projects/*/memory/*.md` and `memory/*.md` — the corpus
- `skills/memory/SKILL.md` — the shape new memories are written in
- `tests/test_resident_census.py` — budgets `MEMORY.md` by name
- `requirements-dev.txt` — `PyYAML` is declared; `python-frontmatter` and
  `pydantic` are not

## Actions

1. Fix the schema: OKF v0.1 fields (`type`, `title`, `description`, `tags`,
   `timestamp`), flat, one nesting level. Drop `name:`. The current split
   between top-level `type:` and `metadata.type` is what the migration removes,
   and it resolves toward the flat spelling.
2. Declare `python-frontmatter` and `pydantic` as pinned dependencies and lock
   them. Read and write with the first; validate with a pydantic model in this
   repo, since **no common OKF validator exists** — that gap is the ecosystem's,
   not this repo's, and the model is small enough to own.
3. Migrate: type the 47, converge the 208, fill `title` from the index line that
   currently holds it, fill `timestamp` from `0917`'s rescued dates, leave
   `tags` empty rather than inventing them.
4. Emit both indexes: `index.md` complete, `MEMORY.md` budgeted with an
   overflow line naming `index.md`.
5. **Validate the `[[slug]]` links.** 533 bodies already carry them and nothing
   has ever resolved them, so the convention is established and its integrity is
   unknown. Report dangling links; do not auto-fix them, since a dangling link
   may mean a body that should exist rather than a typo.

## Test

Red first, and each on a fixture rather than on a count — a count-based
criterion passes vacuously the day after it is written:

- a deliberately untyped body fails the schema check
- a body with `type:` at top level fails, so the old shape cannot come back
- a body whose `[[slug]]` names nothing is reported
- the generator emits a complete `index.md` and a budgeted `MEMORY.md`, on a
  fixture where the two differ — identical output would mean the cut is not
  being applied, or the catalogue is not complete

## Verification

- [ ] Counts before and after, stated in the pull request
- [ ] Every fixture fails without the fix and passes with it
- [ ] A spot-check sample of migrated bodies reads correctly in both consumers
- [ ] `MEMORY.md` is still loaded by the runtime after the change

## Invariants

- No body content changes. This is frontmatter plus a link report.
- No body is deleted.
- `MEMORY.md` keeps being emitted. Anything that stops emitting it removes the
  resident index, which is the channel the whole design is about — but it is the
  alias, not the canonical name.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] One schema, validated by a pydantic model in this repo
- [ ] `python-frontmatter` and `pydantic` pinned and locked
- [ ] Every live body carries the OKF fields, flat, with no `name:`
- [ ] `title` lives in the body; the index derives it rather than holding it
- [ ] `index.md` complete and `MEMORY.md` budgeted, both generated, the
      difference proven on an over-budget fixture
- [ ] The overflow line names `index.md`
- [ ] The convention's completeness expectation is checked and recorded
- [ ] Dangling `[[slug]]` links reported, with a count
